WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.770
As I’m sure you’re aware I’ve just hit
the big one million subscribers!

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:10.440
I can’t believe I’m actually here, it’s
been an amazing journey that I’ll never

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.440
forget.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.530
A few weeks ago I asked you on Facebook to
ask me questions about myself.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:25.210
So here’s another Q&amp;A to celebrate this
gargantuan milestone.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:29.190
Mark asked, “What was your favourite video
out of all the ones you’ve made.”

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.590
I’ve enjoyed making every video, it’s
amazing how interesting even the most mundane

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.820
of topics can be once you learn a bit about
it.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:42.830
But my favourite was “Can You Live Forever?”,
I found researching it absolutely fascinating.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.920
When it comes to technology we live in a very
prosperous time.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.980
There’s so many mind-blowing studies going
on, seeing how far we can push the human body

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.620
and reading about them really interested me.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:59.930
Jack, Harshit, Nupur, Jordan, Cedar and Alex
asked “How long does it take you to make

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.210
a video?”

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:07.299
About 3-6 days depending on the complexity
of the topic, 70% of that is research, the

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:11.580
other 30% is made up of sourcing media, filming
and editing.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.740
Sarah and Christopher asked, “Why do you
wear that certain suit in all your videos?”

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.229
It might seem like it but I don’t always
wear a suit when I’m off-video.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:24.100
I do however, like to dress smart whenever
I can.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.010
If someone goes to the effort of putting a
suit on you know they’re fully committed

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.950
to the task in hand and it shows they mean
business.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.580
Just like if I worked in an office, when I
“show up for work” every day and make

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.920
videos I want to look my absolute best for
you guys.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.180
You’ve gone to the effort of taking ten
minutes out of your day to watch me and that

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.439
means a lot to me, so my way of saying thank
you is looking as good as I possibly can for

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:50.439
you.

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:53.850
It’s not about trying to look intelligent
or authoritative, it’s about giving you

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.670
the best experience I possibly can when you
watch my videos.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:03.520
Efren, Ross, Demix, and Eric asked “What
do you do in your spare time?”.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.740
I have two major hobbies, the first is photography.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:12.150
I’ve been doing amateur photography for
almost ten years now and I even did it professionally

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:17.569
for a short time but the whole business side
of it tainted the enjoyment for me, so now

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:19.640
I do it strictly as a hobby.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.730
My second hobby is cooking.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.230
I love experimenting with food and combining
simple, fresh ingredients.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:31.330
For me cooking is almost a game, I like taking
an ingredient and seeing what crazy things

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.560
I can do to it to really enhance the experience
of eating it.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:40.000
We eat everyday of our lives so we shouldn’t
make eating a chore, it should be something

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.260
we look forward to and get excited about.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.260
Alfred asked “Do you prefer to have facts
around the US or outside the US?”

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.050
When it comes to facts I really don’t care
what country it’s from.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.660
I choose facts based on how interesting they
are and nothing more, it’s as simple as

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.380
that, if the majority of my facts are based
around the US, that’s only because it’s

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.460
an interesting country.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:08.819
Sure at time I tend to focus my figures and
statistics around America, but that’s only

00:03:08.819 --> 00:03:11.450
because the majority of my viewers are American.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:17.420
That’s also why I use dollars as a representative
currency, even though I’m British, because

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.010
dollars are what the majority of my viewers
are use to.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:26.440
I try my best to make my videos friendly and
accessible to people from every country, the

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.459
last thing I want to do is ostracize anyone.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:32.760
Sheikh asked “Why did you decide to become
a YouTuber?

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.471
And where would you be if you werne’t a
YouTuber?”

00:03:36.471 --> 00:03:41.820
I already answered why I became a YouTuber
in my first Q&amp;A, I’ll link to that in the

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.820
description.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.970
But to answer your other question, what I
would be doing if I weren’t a YouTuber?

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:50.600
It’s highly likely I would be a web developer.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.849
When I was thirteen I taught myself basic
HTML mark-up and CSS.

00:03:54.849 --> 00:04:00.450
A few years later and I trained as a graphic
designer in college, but I soon realised I

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.750
was more interested with the code working
behind the scenes of a website than making

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.769
the frontend look pretty.

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:12.099
So whilst I was in college I taught myself
other programming languages such as Python,

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.590
Java and C, mostly out of curiosity to see
what I could do with them.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:19.819
I’ve always found a certain beauty in code.

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:25.210
A few months after I started YouTube I was
offered a web development job in Manchester

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:30.319
(a city in the north of England) and I had
to make the tough decision between a safe

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:36.270
job in a cushy office or continuing making
fascinating videos, which was what I really

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.809
wanted to do.

00:04:37.809 --> 00:04:42.379
This was when my channel was still starting
up and it was very uncertain whether anything

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:44.499
would actually become of it.

00:04:44.499 --> 00:04:47.900
So I took a risk and chose YouTube.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.199
Luckily I made the right decision and boy
am I glad I did, because this whole experience

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:54.819
has been amazing.

00:04:54.819 --> 00:04:57.009
I wouldn’t trade it for anything.

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.990
Bomi asked “Do you play video games?

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.419
If you do, what’s your favourite game?”

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:06.330
Yes of course, as a teenager I used to play
a lot of video games.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:11.100
Unfortunately, I don’t play any more, simply
because it’s hard to find the time.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:16.309
Very, very occasionally I get to kick back
and play but, if I do have spare time, I prefer

00:05:16.309 --> 00:05:21.419
to invest it into YouTube because I get a
much bigger kick out of making videos than

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:23.110
shooting aliens.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:29.249
My all time favourite game is Fallout 3, I
just love the unnerving, dystopian feel to

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:30.249
it.

00:05:30.249 --> 00:05:37.860
Maurice asked “Would you rather fight one
horse-sized duck or 100 duck-sized horses?”

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.639
I’ve put some thought into this and I think
I’d have to go with one horse-sized duck,

00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:47.000
because at least I could always keep it within
my field of vision.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.129
Try doing that with 100 tiny horses, those
sneaky little buggers could be up to anything

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:52.860
behind your back.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:59.069
Keishla, Genese, Arie, Joris and Lorenzo asked
“What music do you listen to?”

00:05:59.069 --> 00:06:02.199
When it comes to music I’m very much stuck
in the past.

00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:07.340
I adore 60s and 70s music, specifically the
British rock bands that were so typical of

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.340
the era.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.849
The Beatles, The Kinks, Led Zepplin etc.

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:16.770
I’m also a big fan of folk-rock names such
as Bob Dylan, Neil Young, Simon and Garfunkel

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.309
and Rodriguez.

00:06:18.309 --> 00:06:22.849
Katie asked “What are your favourite books,
and who are your favourite writers?”

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:26.839
It should come as no surprise that one of
my favourite book series is the Hitchhiker’s

00:06:26.839 --> 00:06:29.190
Guide series by Douglas Adams.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:35.419
But I read very little fiction, I love factual
books, I will happily read a book on any topic,

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:41.479
but I particularly like reading Physics books
about stuff such as black holes, the universe

00:06:41.479 --> 00:06:42.830
and relativity.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:50.330
That being said one of my favourite wordsmiths
is actually a fiction author, F. Scott Fitzgerald.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.979
Best known for his novel The Great Gatsby.

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:58.080
The beautiful poetic prose meticulously crafted
into each and every paragraph stayed with

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.369
me for years after I read it.

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:03.409
Matthew asked “What’s your favourite drink?”

00:07:03.409 --> 00:07:08.289
My favourite drink is coffee, there’s quite
simply nothing better than the smell of freshly

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:10.879
ground coffee beans in the morning.

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:15.599
But being British I also love tea, I know,
so predictable.

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:19.990
In case you were wondering, my favourite alcoholic
beverage is wine.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.389
Liam asked “If you could have a superpower,
what would it be?

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:25.179
And what would you do with it?”

00:07:25.179 --> 00:07:30.059
I would love the power to be able to rapidly
flick through a book and absorb all the knowledge

00:07:30.059 --> 00:07:32.159
inside in a matter of seconds.

00:07:32.159 --> 00:07:35.069
I love learning and that would just be incredible.

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:39.729
Okay I wouldn’t be able to beat up many
villains with that superpower but I would

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:44.490
be able to deploy my superior knowledge to
create computer learning algorithms that predict

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.839
were the villains will strike next then cowardly
warn the police.

00:07:47.839 --> 00:07:53.639
Yeah, that concept definitely needs some polishing
before it’s made into a super-hero flick.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:57.189
Andrew asked “When I was a child, what did
I want to be?”

00:07:57.189 --> 00:08:03.429
When I was really young I wanted to be a scientist
but as I grew up I saw myself going into either

00:08:03.429 --> 00:08:08.809
engineering or computer science, both fields
always interested me and still do.

00:08:08.809 --> 00:08:12.529
But as is usually the case, I ended up doing
neither.

00:08:12.529 --> 00:08:17.709
I really can’t thank you enough for helping
me to reach such an amazing milestone.

00:08:17.709 --> 00:08:22.770
Reaching one million subscribers is by far
the most astonishing think I’ve ever accomplished

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:28.899
in my life, I just really can’t believe
it’s happened to me, so thank you and here’s

00:08:28.899 --> 00:08:36.860
to many more years of Thoughty2.

